WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:07.020
By now, most of you are almost certainly aware&nbsp;
of SCP-343. From the outset, he appears to be an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:12.300
unassuming old man, although the specific details&nbsp;
of his appearance are subject to change based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:17.460
on whoever happens to be looking at him. Oh, and&nbsp;
he’s also God; we probably should have opened with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:24.000
that. Or, at the very least, SCP-343 claims that&nbsp;
he was responsible for creating… well, everything.&nbsp;

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.580
Virtually the entire universe as we know and&nbsp;
understand it, and even all of the parts we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:35.520
can’t even fathom are, according to him,&nbsp;
the result of SCP-343’s design. It’s that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:41.880
assertion that earned him his nickname of ‘God’&nbsp;
among the staff of the SCP Foundation. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:46.320
that and the fact that he can seemingly do&nbsp;
anything with as little as a thought. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:52.740
SCP-343 doesn’t really even need to think all&nbsp;
that hard to literally rework reality around him.&nbsp;

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:58.380
So, you might be wondering, with virtually&nbsp;
unlimited reality-warping power that can enable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:04.200
him to do and be anywhere, all the knowledge in&nbsp;
the entire universe, and nothing but free time on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:09.720
his hands: what exactly to SCP-343 do all day?
6:00 AM&nbsp;

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:16.200
SCP-343 starts most days awake before most&nbsp;
of the Foundation staff at Site 17. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.700
to say he’s awake before is actually&nbsp;
something of a misstatement. Technically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:26.520
he can’t be awake before anybody, because he never&nbsp;
really sleeps to begin with. He doesn’t need to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.900
not only because he doesn’t require sleep to&nbsp;
replenish energy like any mortal being does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:36.420
but also due to the fact that SCP-343&nbsp;
doesn’t exactly experience time in much the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:42.480
same way as human beings do. After all, he did –&nbsp;
supposedly – create the very force of time itself.&nbsp;

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:47.820
Rather than living moment to moment like we do,&nbsp;
experiencing the present, which then becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:55.680
our past, SCP-343 can exist at any and all points&nbsp;
in time at will. For him, the past, present, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:00.960
future are occurring simultaneously, although he&nbsp;
seems to stay as closely tethered to the present,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:05.700
but this is more out of choice.
If he wanted to, we’re sure SCP-343&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:11.040
could be at every moment in history, probably even&nbsp;
in multiple different locations and even timelines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:16.380
at once. But that’s just what happens when you’re&nbsp;
the apparent original architect of the universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:21.720
and don’t rely on ancient numerical systems and&nbsp;
the rotation of the Earth in order to tell time.&nbsp;

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:27.300
After being awake for all of what we&nbsp;
perceive as nighttime, SCP-343 will go for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:31.260
a stroll around nice and early. Oh, you thought we&nbsp;
meant a stroll around the facility he’s kept at?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:38.340
No, no, he’ll take a stroll anywhere, literally&nbsp;
phasing through solid matter and sometimes even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:42.900
just teleporting to whatever place takes his&nbsp;
fancy. Sometimes he’ll just choose a place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:47.160
at random, or pick an old favorite; just&nbsp;
somewhere scenic to watch the sun rise.&nbsp;

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:50.460
8:00 AM
Once the Foundation’s personnel have had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:56.460
enough time to both fully wake up and realize that&nbsp;
SCP-343 has wandered out of his quarters, then an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:01.140
urgent search will undoubtedly be underway.&nbsp;
Panicked Foundation security teams will start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:06.780
racing through the halls of Site 17, frantically&nbsp;
searching for SCP-343. He’s not exactly trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:12.240
to evade them, he’s just naturally very resistant&nbsp;
to being contained. It certainly makes keeping up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:17.340
with him a challenge, especially when he walks&nbsp;
through walls into the cells of other SCPs.&nbsp;

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:21.780
When the security team find him,&nbsp;
it’s inevitably because alarms are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:26.340
sounding to alert them that another containment&nbsp;
chamber has been breached. Sometimes, in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:32.040
more often than not, the staff stationed at SCP&nbsp;
Site 17 will receive word from another Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:38.220
installation that they’ve just seen SCP-343 stroll&nbsp;
past security at a completely different location.&nbsp;

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:42.180
Sometimes he’ll pay a visit&nbsp;
to SCP-049, the Plague Doctor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:47.400
usually casually conversing with the anomalous&nbsp;
physician and admiring his surgical skills.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:53.040
Handling medical tools is a tricky enough task,&nbsp;
made even harder with a beaky mask on your face.&nbsp;

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:58.680
On other occasions, the Foundation has caught up&nbsp;
with SCP-343 on his morning strolls in various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:04.260
other facilities, or with a number of different&nbsp;
anomalies. Often, he’ll slip through walls to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:09.780
spend time with SCP-529, better known as&nbsp;
Josie the Half Cat; an otherwise ordinary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:15.720
domesticated cat with anomalously intangible&nbsp;
hind quarters. Sometimes SCP-343 will also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:22.440
pay a morning visit to SCP-999, stating that he&nbsp;
hopes the Tickle Monster will be ‘ready one day’.&nbsp;

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:28.140
Other times, SCP-343 has been found by the&nbsp;
Foundation sitting with Cain, with the pair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:33.180
usually being in mid-discussion over some deep&nbsp;
philosophical pondering. The pair of them are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:38.400
rather friendly with each other, as if they’ve&nbsp;
known each other for a while, with SCP-343 on one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:43.620
occasion even being noted as remarking to Cain:
“It’s so good to see how far you’ve come, my boy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:46.440
especially after all that nasty&nbsp;
business with your brother.”&nbsp;

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:50.340
10:00 AM
Wherever he ends up, once the Foundation is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:56.820
able to catch up with him, they politely ask him&nbsp;
to return to his quarters back at Site 17. SCP-343&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.820 --> 00:05:01.860
is always amicable and will make his way back&nbsp;
post-haste, usually arriving before the security&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:08.640
team who chased him down are able to make it back.
Once he’s back, it doesn’t take SCP-343 long to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:13.680
get bored, however. So, he decides to undertake&nbsp;
a little bit of home decorating to pass the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:18.120
time. He’s already conjured a fireplace and&nbsp;
plenty of elegant furniture into his room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:22.800
but on this particular day, he feels like&nbsp;
adding some color to brighten the place up.&nbsp;

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:27.180
With little effort, the Mona Lisa, The&nbsp;
Starry Night, The Persistence of Memory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:32.640
Café Terrace at Night, and plenty more of the&nbsp;
world’s most famous paintings all simultaneously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:39.300
appear on the walls. Having summoned so many&nbsp;
famous works to his room, SCP-343 has transformed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:44.340
his quarters into his own personal art gallery.
He spends a while studying the paintings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:48.900
looking at the skillfulness that went into&nbsp;
the creation of each one. Every brush stroke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:54.480
every color choice; he almost seems like a proud&nbsp;
father looking at his child’s art on display at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.480 --> 00:06:01.800
school open day. SCP-343 finds it oddly humbling,&nbsp;
to see how human artists have reflected just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:08.340
fraction of the majestic universe he created, and&nbsp;
all through such simple means. Paint on canvas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:14.220
oil and water, brushes, and an easel; they&nbsp;
seem so quaint to someone whose preferred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:19.500
medium is the very fabric of reality itself.&nbsp;
And yet, so many of the paintings he looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:26.400
at seem to show the world through an even more&nbsp;
beautiful lens. Imagination, SCP-343 admired it.&nbsp;

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:29.820
Naturally, when multiple art galleries&nbsp;
around the world reported that some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:34.560
their most famous works had all been stolen at&nbsp;
the exact same moment, it didn’t take very long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:38.940
for that news to reach the Foundation. When&nbsp;
they were eventually able to determine where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:44.520
the paintings were, a squad of guards politely&nbsp;
told SCP-343 that they needed to be returned.&nbsp;

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:47.820
“We’ve been looking everywhere&nbsp;
for them,” one explained.&nbsp;

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:53.340
With as little as a blink, SCP-343 sent each of&nbsp;
the paintings back to where they had come from.&nbsp;

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:55.980
12:00 PM
At midday, there was a knock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.980 --> 00:07:02.760
at SCP-343’s door, from notorious high-ranking&nbsp;
Foundation researcher Doctor Jack Bright. Often,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:08.580
whenever he was working close enough to Site&nbsp;
17, Doctor Bright would pop in to visit SCP-343,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:14.160
usually bringing along the anomalous reality&nbsp;
warper’s preferred meal: hamburgers. The pair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:18.360
would chat casually to begin with, enjoying&nbsp;
their food while a pair of security guards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:23.280
stood watch outside. However, inevitably their&nbsp;
conversation would always take the same turn…&nbsp;

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:27.240
“You could remove it, couldn’t&nbsp;
you?” Doctor Bright asked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:32.880
referring to SCP-963, the amulet he wore&nbsp;
around his neck. “Or nullify it somehow?”&nbsp;

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:37.380
The anomalous artifact was bound to Jack&nbsp;
Bright, allowing his consciousness to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:42.780
passed from one host body to the next, granting&nbsp;
him functional immortality. Although, over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:49.800
his extended lifespan had felt less and less&nbsp;
like a gift, and more like a curse. SCP-343&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:54.240
had already been aware of this, thanks to them&nbsp;
having the same conversation over a number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:58.320
years. He’d known Doctor Bright for longer&nbsp;
than most others working at the Foundation;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:05.580
and knew how badly the researcher wanted to die.&nbsp;
But every time, SCP-343 would deny Jack’s request.&nbsp;

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:10.920
“Jack, you know I could,” SCP-343 replied.
“But you won’t,” the Foundation doctor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:13.980
finished the sentence. “Why not?&nbsp;
Will you explain that to me?”&nbsp;

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:18.780
“Because I know why you’re asking.”
“And why am I?” Jack probed further,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:23.520
calm, not once raising his voice.
“It’s not that you’re tired of being alive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:30.660
for so long, although that may be how it feels,”&nbsp;
SCP-343 explained. “But you only feel that way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:37.500
because you’re unfulfilled. You have this gift of&nbsp;
immortality, and what do you use it for? You work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:43.200
all you do is work. Just because you no longer&nbsp;
have the short life everyone else doesn’t mean you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:50.340
should stop living, Jack. You devote yourself to&nbsp;
the Foundation, all its SCPs and horrors. Go out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:56.820
and do something for yourself every so often. Go&nbsp;
to a party, meet people. Or make something, create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.820 --> 00:09:02.040
a piece of art. You could even try making a movie.&nbsp;
What’s the worst that could happen? That’s why I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:07.620
won’t remove the amulet, Jack, because I want&nbsp;
you to have a chance to enjoy your extra time.”&nbsp;

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:10.080
1:30 PM
After finishing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:16.260
his lunch with Doctor Bright, SCP-343 takes some&nbsp;
time out of his day for recreation. Completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:22.860
unbeknownst to the Foundation, SCP-343 phases out&nbsp;
of reality – as we understand it – and enters an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:29.100
extradimensional space, an astral plane, some&nbsp;
might call it. While there, SCP-343 likes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:34.140
enjoy a game of Solitaire, conjuring a deck of&nbsp;
playing cards from nothing and using them to play.&nbsp;

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:39.720
Solitaire is a basic game, and once based&nbsp;
on chance. How easy or difficult it is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:44.580
complete rows of cards all in their matching&nbsp;
set, is all down to the random arrangement of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:51.420
the deck. That could be why SCP-343 likes playing&nbsp;
a game like Solitaire. He has the ability – so he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:57.120
claims – to create or unmake any part of the&nbsp;
universe, to go anywhere, or to do anything.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:01.680
If he wanted to, the cards could always be in&nbsp;
the perfect order for him to finish the game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:08.100
in a few seconds. But where would be the fun?
Most, if not all, of SCP-343’s behavior seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:13.620
to imply that – despite his untold levels of&nbsp;
reality-warping power – he enjoys giving up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:19.500
control. Or at least, the illusion of not having&nbsp;
to control everything around him. Playing with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:24.480
completely random deck of cards feels very much&nbsp;
in line with that aspect of his personality.&nbsp;

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:31.260
If SCP-343 wanted to, he could always draw the&nbsp;
right card he needed for his Solitaire games, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:36.360
then he would be giving up that illusion of not&nbsp;
having control. In much the same vein, he could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:42.720
very easily erase the universe’s most dangerous&nbsp;
anomalies, or even destroy the SCP Foundation with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:48.600
little effort, if he wanted to. And yet, he opts&nbsp;
instead to go through the motions, to let the deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:51.720
of cards stay in random order.
4:00 PM&nbsp;

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:55.740
By the time the afternoon is about to&nbsp;
become the evening, there’s normally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.740 --> 00:11:03.240
a bit of commotion at Site 17, all caused by&nbsp;
SCP-343. Given how large the SCP Foundation is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:08.700
and how expansive the organization’s various&nbsp;
facilities and operations are, inevitably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:14.820
someone who hasn’t heard about SCP-343 eventually&nbsp;
learns of his existence. When another group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:19.920
within the Foundation learns that Site 17 is&nbsp;
accommodating a potentially dangerous SCP,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:25.680
with the ability to warp reality on a completely&nbsp;
unknown scale, it tends to cause a little panic.&nbsp;

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:30.120
Requestioning a Mobile Task Force, a Site&nbsp;
Director from another facility stormed into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:37.020
Site 17. He demanded to be shown to SCP-343’s&nbsp;
quarters, despite protests from the on-site staff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.020 --> 00:11:40.980
who all reassured the Director that there&nbsp;
was nothing to worry about. They had all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:47.220
worked with SCP-343, all conversed with him,&nbsp;
and knew him to be benevolent. But that just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:53.460
gave the Site Director more cause for concern.&nbsp;
If SCP-343 could really do anything, surely he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:58.980
could have influenced how the personnel at Site&nbsp;
17 perceived him, via some cognito hazardous means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:05.280
that meant they only thought he was harmless.
Bursting into SCP-343’s room with the MTF&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:10.140
troopers, the Site Director demanded that the&nbsp;
powerful reality warper explain himself. By&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:15.540
this point, this had happened enough times that&nbsp;
SCP-343 just used the sudden intrusion as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.540 --> 00:12:20.220
excuse to leave the facility again for a while.&nbsp;
He never even acknowledged that the agents from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:24.780
another part of the Foundation were trying&nbsp;
to confine him, he merely got up and walked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.780 --> 00:12:29.220
away. Even when the Site Director, who was a&nbsp;
far more trigger-happy one this time around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:36.120
commanded his men to open fire, SCP-343 was&nbsp;
unphased. The bullets from the MTF’s weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:41.760
had no effect, melting into nothing practically&nbsp;
the second they exited the barrel of their guns.&nbsp;

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:47.640
Ignoring angered shouts to sit back down&nbsp;
and stay put, SCP-343 casually walked off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:53.100
disappearing through a wall and leaving the Site&nbsp;
Director and MTF scrabbling to track down where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:55.320
he’d gone to now.
7:00 PM&nbsp;

00:12:55.320 --> 00:13:01.000
The wider SCP Foundation was thrown into a state&nbsp;
of panic, all while the reassurances of the Site&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:06.900
17 staff fell on deaf ears. It didn’t matter&nbsp;
how many times they said that SCP-343 would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:11.280
come back in his own time, the Foundation&nbsp;
were already scouring the Earth looking for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:16.200
the reality-warping old man. Of course, few&nbsp;
people think to look for you when you hide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:23.460
out in a specific quiet old town in Belgium.
SCP-343 had decided to take a sightseeing tour&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:28.920
of Europe while the SCP Foundation attempted&nbsp;
to track him down. Every time he sensed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.920 --> 00:13:34.320
their agents were nearby, he vanished to another&nbsp;
scenic location. He enjoyed being around people;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:39.660
their lives were so short and fleeting compared&nbsp;
to his, the novelty of spending time walking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:45.720
among his ‘creations’ never seemed to wear thin.
They spent their lives fretting over concerns that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:50.280
seemed so trivial to a being who could see&nbsp;
the entire cosmic scope of everything that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:55.440
existed. And yet, it was these ordinary people&nbsp;
who had built great cities and civilizations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.440 --> 00:14:01.980
who had developed culture and art beyond anything&nbsp;
SCP-343 could ever have anticipated when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:09.000
created them… if he actually did create them.
His tour across Europe had brought SCP-343&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:14.400
to Bruges, the oldest and most well-preserved&nbsp;
medieval town in Belgium. He stood at the top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:18.840
of an old clock tower, looking out at the view&nbsp;
of the buildings and the canal that ran through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:24.720
Bruges. He was impressed at how long a place like&nbsp;
this, made by man, had stood the test of time.&nbsp;

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:31.080
An old man came to join SCP-343, having observed&nbsp;
him in the tower and thinking he looked lonely.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:36.120
The pair of them got to chatting, the reality&nbsp;
warper revealed who he was; much to the man’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:40.260
surprise, he found himself believing the&nbsp;
outlandish claim that this stranger had indeed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.260 --> 00:14:45.240
made the whole universe. The old man proceeded to&nbsp;
ask about the meaning of life and the universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:51.180
what everything meant and was for.
“Forty-two,” SCP-343 answered smirking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.180 --> 00:14:54.660
still staring out over Bruges.
The old man looked confused.&nbsp;

00:14:55.200 --> 00:14:58.560
“That’s just a joke from a book I quite enjoyed.”
8:00 PM&nbsp;

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:04.740
SCP-343’s sightseeing break didn’t end in&nbsp;
Belgium. In fact, it didn’t even stay confined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:10.140
to Earth for very long. The SCP Foundation,&nbsp;
the ones who weren’t stationed at Site 17,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.140 --> 00:15:15.060
were still on the lookout for the reality-warping&nbsp;
old man, although they were still yet to turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:22.680
their gaze upwards. Without any fanfare, nor any&nbsp;
explosive teleportation, SCP-343 just popped into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:27.360
existence… standing on the surface of the Moon.
He basked in the Earth-light for a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:33.300
looking at the view of the entire world. From&nbsp;
here, it seemed so much smaller, still colossal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:40.200
in size compared to SCP-343 himself, but it was&nbsp;
the distance. Everything seemed so far away, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.200 --> 00:15:47.880
microscopic that for a mortal, it might be hard to&nbsp;
comprehend. But for SCP-343, he could see clearly:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.880 --> 00:15:54.120
a world teeming with life. He turned his gaze to&nbsp;
the infinite expanse of space, the whole universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:59.580
beyond; stars burning against an inky blackness.
As he stood looking out at the solar system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.580 --> 00:16:03.780
like someone admiring their handiwork, he&nbsp;
spotted something that surprised even him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:10.680
A few million kilometers from the Sun was an&nbsp;
old Volkswagen microbus. This was SCP-1958,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.680 --> 00:16:15.360
a vehicle that had been heavily modified by&nbsp;
a group of friends so that they could leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:19.620
Earth and go travel the cosmos together.&nbsp;
They had initially been successful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:24.540
and had planned to visit Alpha Centauri, a&nbsp;
Star System in the Constellation of Centaurus.&nbsp;

00:16:24.540 --> 00:16:28.920
Unfortunately, a miscalculation meant it&nbsp;
would take millions of years to reach their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.920 --> 00:16:35.700
destination. Ultimately, the entire group on&nbsp;
board SCP-1958 had perished, lost in space.&nbsp;

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:41.220
While he was saddened to know of their deaths&nbsp;
– thanks to his omniscience – SCP-343 could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:45.780
hardly blame the group of young friends for&nbsp;
wanting to travel to the stars. He might not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.780 --> 00:16:50.160
have chosen to do so in an old microbus,&nbsp;
but he could understand why they would go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:55.500
to such lengths to fly away from Earth.&nbsp;
It truly was beautiful up here in space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.500 --> 00:16:58.620
SCP-343 thought.
10:00 PM&nbsp;

00:16:58.620 --> 00:17:04.500
Having decided he’d been gone for long enough,&nbsp;
SCP-343 thought it was about time that he should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:10.260
be making his way back to Site 17. He’d given the&nbsp;
SCP Foundation enough of a run around for one day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.260 --> 00:17:17.040
SCP-343 would finally settle back into the comfort&nbsp;
of his cell – exactly where he wanted to be.&nbsp;

